 trying to help him like defeat  dieter and angel and the film ends like  a little bit ambiguously because i'm not  entirely sure like jesus isn't really  sure either if destroying the device  will kill him or will return him to  normal and you don't really know like  which of those things is gonna happen so  i will say that even though kronos isn't  quite as i guess self-assured or  polished as guillermo del toro's later  films were which like i said not a big  surprise because i think it only cost  million and a half two million to make  which you know only two million dollars  but yeah for a movie that's not much and  it was his first movie and he was like  really young but it's so it's like  surprisingly assured for that you know  for being like his first film and  everything but it's still like a really  i mean it's an extraordinary debut  really i mean it's a dark fantasy i  guess it's it's a horror movie but i  would call it a little bit more like on  the dark fantasy end but it does have  quite a bit of gore it's has like a  really original take on the vampire  mythos as i said and it has like a  really strong emotional core at center  about you know the unconditional love  between like aurora like even though  like still loving her grandfather and  still helping him even though he's  turning into a monster and also has some  stuff to say about maybe like the  poisonous lure of immortality like what  you'll give up to get that  and i will say that even though jesus uh  you know the main character he falls  victim to the kronos device accidentally  like he doesn't know what it is he just  finds it in the bottom of thing he's  like oh neat and then like you know he  essentially just gets turned into a  vampire like by accident but he ends up  becoming like something of a slave to it  um but he's still like sympathetic uh  throughout the whole entire movie even  as he  you know toward the end like he starts  struggling with his desire to like drink  the blood o